# Add fonts

This file lives under `.agents/skills/add-fonts/`, following the [Agent Skills convention][agent-skills]
— many coding agents (Claude Code, Codex CLI, and others) auto-discover and load skills from
`.agents/skills/**/SKILL.md` without any tool-specific adapter file. A Claude-Code-specific pointer
to this file lives at `.claude/skills/add-fonts/SKILL.md`.

[agent-skills]: https://agentskills.io/specification

## Scope — what this skill assumes about the app

This app was generated by `@ronas-it/nx-generators`' `next-app` generator (with or without
`--withMantine`). That means `apps/<app>/app/` is the Next.js App Router root — this app's actual
`<html>` element may live one level deeper, e.g. `apps/<app>/app/[locale]/layout.tsx`, for i18n
routing. If more than one app under `apps/` matches, ask the user which one before doing anything.

`clsx` is already a dependency in this repo — use it to combine a font's `.variable` className
with any existing className on the `<html>` element rather than string-concatenating (see the
UI-kit's `Icon` component for the existing usage convention, if one exists).

## Progress reporting (required)

If you're running standalone (not handed off to from `import-figma-ui`), post a short plan up
front — **Font names → Availability check → Layout wiring → Theme wiring** — and keep it current
the same way described in `import-figma-ui`'s "Progress reporting" section.

## Phase 1 — Determine the font names

- **If you were handed a Figma link/access already** (i.e. you're running as part of
  `import-figma-ui`, or the user gave you a Figma link directly): locate the "Variables" block's
  Typekit/typography table and read its typeface rows — the ones naming actual font families
  rather than sizes, typically labeled ending in "Primary" and "Secondary" (e.g. a row path like
  `Typeface / Primary`). Read off the font family name each one points to (e.g. "Inter", "Roboto
  Slab"). There may be more than two (e.g. a monospace face for code) — read whatever rows are
  actually there rather than assuming exactly two.
- **Otherwise**, ask the user which font(s) to add and, if there's more than one, what each is for
  (body text vs. headings vs. something else) — you need that to know which CSS variable/theme
  field each one should end up wired to.

## Phase 2 — Check Google Fonts availability

For each font name, check whether `next/font/google` ships it. From the repo root (where `next`
is actually installed), run:

```sh
node -e "console.log(Object.keys(require('next/dist/compiled/@next/font/dist/google/font-data.json')))"
```

and check for an exact (case-sensitive) match of the display name. If the workspace layout means
`next` isn't hoisted to the repo root's `node_modules`, run the same command from wherever `next`
actually resolves (e.g. `apps/<app>` if it has its own `node_modules`).

**If a font isn't available**, do not substitute a different font or silently skip it — tell the
user explicitly which typeface(s) need to be installed manually (e.g. self-hosted via
`next/font/local`, or licensed and added as static font files) and that this skill can't do that
part for them. Continue wiring up whichever fonts *are* available.

## Phase 3 — Wire into the layout

For each available font, in the app's root layout (wherever the `<html>` element for this app
actually lives): import it from `next/font/google` per [Next.js's font
docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/fonts) — the export name replaces spaces with
underscores, e.g. "Roboto Mono" → `Roboto_Mono` — and instantiate it with a `variable` matching its
role (e.g. `variable: '--font-primary'` for a "Primary"/body typeface, `--font-secondary` for a
"Secondary"/heading typeface, or whatever name fits if the roles aren't literally "Primary"/
"Secondary"). Apply every font's `.variable` to the `<html>` element's `className`, combined with
`clsx` (see Scope above).

## Phase 4 — Wire into the Mantine theme (if applicable)

Check whether `libs/<app>/shared/ui/ui-kit/src/lib/theme.tsx` (or `theme.ts`) exists. If it
doesn't, this app doesn't use Mantine — skip this phase and note that in the finishing summary.

If it exists:

1. Set `theme.fontFamily` to `var(--font-primary)` (or whichever CSS variable you gave the
   body/default typeface in Phase 3). The generator ships this commented out:
   ```ts
   // TODO: add your font here
   // fontFamily: 'Inter',
   ```
   Replace it outright rather than leaving the TODO comment behind.
2. **If there's a distinct heading/display typeface** (e.g. a "Secondary" face), check whether the
   Typekit table's heading composites (if you have Figma access — look for rows like `title/H1`
   through `title/H5` or similar) actually reference it. Mantine's `headings.fontFamily` is a
   single override for all heading levels, not per-level:
   - If the heading composites consistently use the secondary typeface, set
     `theme.headings.fontFamily` to `var(--font-secondary)` (or whatever variable you used).
   - If they're mixed (some heading levels use the primary typeface, others the secondary — this
     happens, don't assume it won't), setting one override for all of them would be wrong. Leave
     `theme.headings.fontFamily` unset and say so explicitly in the finishing summary as a manual
     follow-up, rather than guessing.
   - If you don't have Figma access in this run (fonts were given directly by the user), skip this
     step and say so in the finishing summary — don't invent which typeface headings should use.
3. Verify: `theme.tsx` is TypeScript, so a typo fails loudly. Run `npm run lint` (or the repo's
   equivalent `tsc`/`eslint` script, check `package.json` if the name differs) from the repo root
   and fix anything it flags before finishing.

## Finishing up

Summarize: which fonts were wired up vs. flagged as needing manual install, which CSS variables
they're exposed as, whether `theme.fontFamily`/`theme.headings.fontFamily` were set (and why not,
if skipped).
